Deep in the dungeon there are hideous foes, creatures of unfathomable
menace, beings of utmost spite.  While some of them, such as the great
Wizard of Yendor himself, are members of a species, many of them
belong to no kind, and are the sole members of their type.

These are "uniques" as they currently stand.  Until the Quest, you'll
see none of them, excepting the Oracle.


My idea (this time - and don't think this is my last): "Common"
uniques, which are not native to any special level but appear
(infrequently) in the basic level generation.  Unlike the special
unique demon lords and princes, nemeses and such that appear later,
they would not be beings of great power.  They would all be "named"
versions of already-existing monsters, with customized, non-random
stats, an only semi-random inventory, and only modestly greater
strength than the typical monsters of class.

Like the "true" uniques deep in the dungeon, they could only ever
appear once in a game, though they would show up sufficiently rarely
that the chances of seeing more than, say, three in one game would be
slim.  Each would carry a specific name, allowing players to tell what
they're up against at a glance.

Not that all of these uniques would be hostile.  Many would be player
monsters, and those of the same class would frequently be peaceful. 
They would have customized #chat messages, and they would carry custom
descriptions that would fill the player in on background information
on that particular individual creature.

An example:

----------------------------------------

"Wendy the water nymph has fallen on hard times.  Her days of cycling
between sleeping in dungeon rooms, primping in her mirror, and robbing
dungeon explorers of their hard-won possessions came to an end when
one of those explorers, a wand collector, zapped her with one of his
cancellation-bearing wares.

"While that explorer found his own demise before he could finish her
off, Wendy has often wished that adventurer had killed her – a plain
nymph, deprived of that primary attribute of nymphness, unearthly
beauty, is in a sorry state.  All of the other nymphs used to laugh
and call her names.  They never let poor Wendy join in any nymphoid
games.

But more recently she has regained some measure of self-esteem,
primarily arising from the one item she managed to steal from the
now-deceased source of her problems before that life-altering zap: a
wand of fire."

In game terms, Wendy would be pre-cancelled but in all other respects
a water nymph.  She would carry a wand of fire with a diminished
number of charges (she's already taken care of her snobbish friends),
and know how to use it.

----------------------------------------

Unlike Wendy, not all of the common-uniques would carry special
equipment.  Far from it, many of these monsters are significant
primarily from their stats, or from some event in their
dungeon-dwelling lives that has made them special, such as (as with
Wendy) cancellation, or polymorph, or being summoned by
reverse-genocide or water demon hell-p, or one of the Wizard's
previous pet summonings that stopped a previous adventurer, or even a
shopkeeper looking for a place to set up business.

Player monsters would carry a small portion of that race's starting
equipment plus additional random items relative to depth.  Since
player monsters are theoretically other adventurers in the dungeon,
also searching for the amulet, and thus supposedly going through a
similar exploration process, their equipment would be slightly more
likely to be cursed than usual – worn cursed items often stick around
for a little while, so they would be a bit disproportionately-likely
to be carried by these monsters.

What this patch is not meant to accomplish: push Nethack along the
road of Angbandness.  Towards this end, common-unique equipment would
not be overly unbalancing.  These creatures are not intended to be big
challenges, with commensurate rewards for defeating them, but more
whimsical, giving the player novel situations to overcome.

What this patch would add to the game: running into common-uniques
would help to make the early-to-mid game a little more interesting. 
Nethack players tend to synthesize their games into running
narratives, often telling these stories in the form of YASD posts or
YAAPs.  Intersecting these tales with the quirky, personal-level
stories of monsters living in the mazes seems like it would be
interesting to people.  It is to me, at least.

I'm considering implementing this patch myself.  I'd certainly be open
to suggestions as for common-uniques to put in the game.  Also, I'd
like some advice as to how "creative" they should be.  Wendy is
obviously a figment of my imagination, while most things that make it
into Nethack tends to be borrowed from actual myths, popular
literature, or even real life.  Left to my own devices, I'm more
likely to make up stuff like Wendy, without such basis.

